title Mycelium Cord r3 — the Cord travels as a format cord-dag-v1 record
prompt take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first; approve own design rounds; send each finished increment as its own signed round; STOP at custody gates
think r2 quarantined the equivocator; the design read named r3: the Cord travels. A consensus a developer cannot hand to another node is only a sandbox — a mesh becomes a network when one node's agreed order crosses to a second as readable bytes. Built mycelium/cord_bron.rye composing r1 public API (commit · add · address).
think Format cord-dag-v1: one `block` line per block in COMMITTED order (author key hex · round · kind · amount · star field · body field · fact-signer key hex · fact signature hex · block signature hex), each block's parents as following `parent <hash-hex>` lines. Rendering in committed order means the record lists parents before children, so re-adding in file order satisfies r1's DanglingParent gate.
think The whole block must ride so the recovered block is byte-identical: the block's canonical bytes (author_pk · round · parent_count · parents · fact.sig) determine its address and block signature, and the fold re-verifies the FACT signature over the fact's own content (kind · amount · star · body · signer). So I carry both signatures and every fact field. Star and body are hex-encoded with a `-` sentinel for empty (the Cord's demo facts carry memos like genesis-a as bodies — unlike ledger facts which had body_len 0), so any bytes travel and no space-splitting breaks a token.
think The r3 crux (the headline over a number-only roster): the agreed ORDER travels with the bytes. Proven on metal — render the committed 4-block Dag, parse back, rebuild by re-admitting each block under r1's own add (every signature re-verified, every parent must already be held), commit the rebuilt Dag, and assert the committed order (by block-address bytes) is byte-identical to the original and the fold state identical (supply 700, one star). Re-rendering the parsed record reproduces the exact bytes (a fixed point). A tampered block signature flipped in the record refuses BadSignature at add on rebuild — corruption can never cross as consensus.
think Reader is a purpose-built bounded flat-Bron reader with a small state machine (a pending block accumulates parent lines until the next block line or EOF). Refusals real: BadFormat (header), UnknownField (never dropped), BadLine (token count), BadInt, BadHex, ParentBeforeBlock (a parent line with no current block), TooManyParents (fan-in past r1's bound), TooManyBlocks. Added a mycelium/parse_int.rye symlink to ../tally/parse_int.rye (matching the existing kumara/tally_copy symlink pattern) so the integer parse uses the tree's own bounded parser, not std.fmt.parseInt.
obs GREEN first pass — no red booked. TAME bans clean, width-check exit 0. Additive: r1 (deterministic commit) and r2 (Byzantine refusal) both re-run GREEN under the r3 witness; neither module changed.
loom witness=mycelium_cord_bron_witness blocks=4 record_bytes=n1_fixed_point order_identical=true supply=700 stars=1 tamper=BadSignature first_pass=green
file mycelium/cord_bron.rye r3 — from_dag/render/parse/rebuild; the agreed order travels as a format cord-dag-v1 record byte-for-byte, tamper refused on rebuild
file mycelium/parse_int.rye symlink to ../tally/parse_int.rye (bounded integer parse for the reader)
file tools/mycelium_cord_bron_witness.rish r3 witness; re-runs r1 and r2 GREEN at its door (additive)
recommend keep-going Cord r4 — read a real on-disk Cord fixture true, cross-check the committed order against an independent measure (an awk/rish topological reading of the same file) — two tools, one answer — closing the Cord's agent-doable arc. A Cord gossiped over the wire reaches the serve gate; breach renames (cairn-first) + deep debride + seed force-push stay Keaton's hand.
